# Next.js

## Mục tiêu

Next.js là React framework được sử dụng rộng rãi cho việc xây dựng các web applications hiện đại với features như Server-Side Rendering (SSR), Static Site Generation (SSG), và API routes. Trong Cursor Enterprise Framework, Next.js đóng vai trò là primary frontend framework cho các dự án cần SEO tốt, performance cao, và developer experience tuyệt vời. Mục tiêu của rule này là establish consistent patterns và best practices cho việc sử dụng Next.js trong enterprise projects.

Framework này cung cấp nhiều rendering strategies và data fetching patterns. Việc chọn đúng approach cho từng use case là critical cho performance và maintainability. Rule này sẽ guide developers qua các decisions từ project setup, routing, data fetching, đến deployment.

## Nguyên tắc

**1. Rendering Strategy Selection**

SSG cho static content (homepage, blog posts). SSR cho dynamic, personalized content. ISR cho content that changes but can be cached. CSR cho highly interactive, user-specific content. Choose based on data freshness requirements. Consider SEO implications.

**2. App Router vs Pages Router**

Use App Router for new projects. It's the future of Next.js. Better data fetching with Server Components. Built-in layouts and nested routing. More flexible rendering options. Pages Router still supported but App Router preferred.

**3. Server Components by Default**

Start with Server Components. Move to Client Components only when needed. Interactive UI (useState, useEffect). Browser APIs. Real-time data. This reduces client-side JavaScript.

**4. Data Fetching Patterns**

Use async/await in Server Components. Fetch data close to where it's used. Implement proper caching strategies. Use React cache() for request deduplication. Handle loading and error states properly.

**5. Route Organization**

Group related routes together. Use route groups for layout organization. Collocate routes with their components. Keep pages directory structure logical. Use dynamic routes for parameterized URLs.

**6. API Routes for BFF**

Use API routes as Backend-For-Frontend. Aggregate data from multiple sources. Transform data for frontend needs. Handle authentication/authorization. Keep API routes thin.

**7. Image Optimization**

Use next/image for all images. Specify dimensions when known. Use proper image formats (WebP, AVIF). Implement lazy loading. Generate responsive sizes.

**8. Metadata Management**

Use Metadata API for SEO. Configure Open Graph images. Set proper title templates. Manage sitemap automatically. Structured data for rich results.

**9. State Management**

Server state vs client state. Use URL state when possible. React Query for server state. Context for cross-component state. Minimize client-side state.

**10. Performance Optimization**

Monitor Core Web Vitals. Implement code splitting. Bundle optimization. Font optimization with next/font. Analyze bundle size regularly.

## Checklist

- [ ] Next.js version selected (latest stable)
- [ ] Rendering strategy defined per page
- [ ] App Router structure established
- [ ] Data fetching patterns implemented
- [ ] Image optimization configured
- [ ] Metadata management setup
- [ ] Error handling implemented
- [ ] Loading states designed
- [ ] Testing strategy defined
- [ ] Deployment configuration ready

## Anti Pattern

**1. Overusing Client Components**

Marking everything as 'use client'. Unnecessary JavaScript on client. Slower page loads. Higher bundle size. Use Server Components by default.

**2. Data Fetching in Client Components**

Fetching data on client when server fetch possible. Unnecessary loading states. SEO impact. Performance degradation.

**3. Ignoring Loading States**

Not implementing proper loading UI. Layout shifts. Poor user experience. Use Suspense boundaries.

**4. Improper Error Boundaries**

Not handling errors gracefully. Broken pages. No recovery options. Use error.tsx files.

**5. Large Bundle Sizes**

Importing entire libraries. No tree shaking. Large initial load. Optimize imports carefully.

